\chapter{Directed Graphs: Structure, Traversal, and DOT}
\label{ch:directed-graphs}

Almost every non-trivial domain-specific language has a graph hiding inside
it. A dependency graph says which module must be built before which. A
program graph says which instruction can reach which. A term graph, which
is the subject of \chapref{ch:terms-and-reduction}, says which subterm
occurs inside which. What these have in common is that they are
\emph{shared}: the same node appears in several places at once, the same
edge is traversed many times, and the identity of a node has to survive
being referred to from somewhere else. A tree cannot represent that, and
rebuilding a tree whenever the graph changes is not viable.

DSLtk therefore provides a general-purpose directed graph. It is not
attached to any of the language-construction machinery: the feature tag
\code{dsl::Graphs} is an empty marker with no mixin at all, and
\code{dsl::DirectedGraph} is a free-standing class that a program uses
directly, like \code{std::vector}. That is a deliberate choice. A graph is
a data structure, not a language feature, and the facilities that need one
--- the term graph of the next chapter, for instance --- use it as an
ordinary library type.

The class is generic over two payload types, so it can carry any
information the application wants on its nodes and its edges. The default
is \code{std::string} for both. The rest of this chapter is about the three
things that make the implementation interesting: the slot-based storage,
which gives identifiers a lifetime guarantee that most graph libraries do
not offer; the traversal routines, which are written to be iterative and to
have precisely defined orders; and the \code{redirect} operation, which is
the primitive that makes in-place rewriting possible.

\section{The graph and the rest of the toolkit}

It is worth being explicit about what \code{DirectedGraph} is not. DSLtk
defines four feature tags in this area --- \code{dsl::Graphs},
\code{dsl::Terms}, \code{dsl::TermRewriting} and \code{dsl::GraphReduction}
--- and every one of them is an empty marker: a struct with a single empty
\code{Mixin} template and no members at all. None of them contributes
anything when it is mixed in, and none of them can be used to extend a DSL
class.

This is not an oversight but a deliberate statement about the relationship
between these facilities and the language-construction machinery. The PEG
subsystem, the rewrite feature and the expression template mixin are all
\emph{opt-in components} of a DSL: a class opts in by listing a feature tag
in its base clause, and the mixin then supplies members and operators. The
graph is not like that. It is a data structure that a program uses
directly, exactly as it would use \code{std::unordered_map}. Mixing it
into a DSL would give every DSL an \code{add_node} member and a name
collision with whatever the user had wanted to call it.

The consequence is that the graph has to be good enough on its own rather
than good enough as a component of something else. That is why it has
stable identifiers, deterministic traversal orders, and a DOT exporter: it
has to earn its place.

\section{A worked example}

The program below builds a small dependency graph, computes a topological
order and a shortest path, runs both traversals, prints the graph as DOT,
and then removes a node to show that identifiers are never recycled.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Building a dependency graph and querying it.},label={lst:ch35-graph}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int
main ()
{
  dsl::DirectedGraph<std::string, std::string> graph;

  auto core = graph.add_node ("core");
  auto net = graph.add_node ("net");
  auto util = graph.add_node ("util");
  auto cli = graph.add_node ("cli");
  auto doc = graph.add_node ("doc");

  graph.add_edge (cli, core, "needs");
  graph.add_edge (cli, net, "needs");
  graph.add_edge (core, util, "needs");
  graph.add_edge (net, util, "needs");
  graph.add_edge (doc, util, "needs");

  std::cout << "nodes: " << graph.node_count ()
            << ", edges: " << graph.edge_count () << '\n';

  std::cout << "topological order:";
  for (auto id : graph.topological_sort ())
    std::cout << ' ' << graph.value (id);
  std::cout << '\n';

  if (auto path = graph.shortest_path (cli, util))
    {
      std::cout << "shortest path:";
      for (auto id : *path)
        std::cout << ' ' << graph.value (id);
      std::cout << '\n';
    }

  std::cout << "breadth first from cli:";
  for (auto id : graph.breadth_first (cli))
    std::cout << ' ' << graph.value (id);
  std::cout << '\n';

  std::cout << "depth first from cli:";
  for (auto id : graph.depth_first (cli))
    std::cout << ' ' << graph.value (id);
  std::cout << '\n';

  std::cout << "post-order depth first from cli:";
  for (auto id : graph.depth_first (cli, true))
    std::cout << ' ' << graph.value (id);
  std::cout << '\n';

  std::cout << "strongly connected components: " << graph.strongly_connected_components ().size ()
            << '\n';

  std::cout << graph.to_dot ("dependencies");

  // Removing a node leaves a hole: identifiers are never recycled.
  graph.remove_node (net);
  auto late = graph.add_node ("late");
  std::cout << "after removing net: node_count = " << graph.node_count ()
            << ", the new node's id is " << late << '\n';

  // dot_quote escapes the characters that would otherwise break a label.
  std::cout << "quoted: " << dsl::dot_quote ("a \"quote\" and a\nnewline")
            << '\n';

  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch35-graph}.},label={lst:ch35-output}]
nodes: 5, edges: 5
topological order: cli doc core net util
shortest path: cli core util
breadth first from cli: cli core net util
depth first from cli: cli core util net
post-order depth first from cli: util core net cli
strongly connected components: 5
digraph "dependencies" {
  n0 [label="core"];
  n1 [label="net"];
  n2 [label="util"];
  n3 [label="cli"];
  n4 [label="doc"];
  n3 -> n0 [label="needs"];
  n3 -> n1 [label="needs"];
  n0 -> n2 [label="needs"];
  n1 -> n2 [label="needs"];
  n4 -> n2 [label="needs"];
}
after removing net: node_count = 5, the new node's id is 5
quoted: "a \"quote\" and a\nnewline"
\end{lstlisting}

Two of those lines are worth reading closely.

The depth-first traversals differ in more than order. The pre-order
traversal from \code{cli} visits \code{cli}, \code{core}, \code{util},
\code{net}: it goes as deep as it can before backtracking. The post-order
traversal visits \code{util}, \code{core}, \code{net}, \code{cli}: each node
is reported only after all of its descendants. The two orders are the
standard ones, but they are not the same order, and a program that uses one
where it needs the other will produce subtly wrong answers.

The last line shows the identifier guarantee. After \code{net} --- which had
identifier 1 --- was removed, the graph still had four live nodes; adding
one more produced identifier 5, not 1. The slot that \code{net} occupied is
still there, holding nothing. This is what makes the graph safe to use as
the backing store for a structure in which identifiers are handed out and
referred to over a long period, such as the term graph of the next
chapter.

