The graph stores its nodes in a \code{std::vector<std::optional<Node>>} and
its edges in a \code{std::vector<std::optional<Edge>>}. A node identifier is
simply the index of its slot, and an edge identifier is the index of its
own slot. Each live node keeps two vectors of incident edge identifiers,
one for the edges that point into it and one for the edges that point out
of it.

Three consequences follow, and all three are visible in
Figure~\ref{fig:ch35-slots}.

First, identifiers are \emph{stable}. A node's identifier never changes
while the node lives, and it is never handed to a different node. This is
the guarantee that makes the graph safe to use as the backing store for a
structure whose sub-components are referred to by identifier --- which is
exactly what the term graph of the next chapter does.

Second, identifiers are \emph{not recycled}. Removing a node resets its
slot and leaves a hole. The vector grows monotonically in the number of
nodes that have ever been added, and the identifiers of a long-lived graph
become sparse. This is a deliberate trade: the guarantee is worth more than
the memory, because the alternative --- reusing slots -- would silently
invalidate every identifier a caller was holding.

Third, the dense accessors skip the holes. \code{nodes ()} and
\code{edges ()} return only the live identifiers, in ascending order, and
\code{node_count ()} and \code{edge_count ()} are their sizes. The
identifier space itself is sparse.

The error behaviour is uniform. \code{value}, \code{set_value},
\code{incoming} and \code{outgoing} throw
\code{std::out_of_range} with the message \code{"invalid graph node"} for
an identifier that does not refer to a live node; \code{edge} throws
\code{std::out_of_range} with the message \code{"invalid graph edge"};
and \code{add_edge} validates both endpoints before it does anything.

\section{Traversal}

Both traversals start from a root and visit only the nodes reachable from
it. They return a vector of identifiers.

\code{breadth_first} is the simpler of the two. It uses the result vector
itself as the queue, keeping an index into it, and marks nodes as seen at
the moment they are enqueued. The order is therefore the textbook
breadth-first order: all the nodes at distance one, then all the nodes at
distance two, and so on.

\code{depth_first} is written with an explicit stack of pairs, and the
pair is what makes it support both orders. The first component of the pair
is a node identifier; the second is a flag saying whether the node has
already been expanded. The flag is what allows post-order to be expressed
without recursion.

\begin{pseudocode}[Depth-first search]
\textbf{function} depth\_first (root, postorder)\\
\quad pending = a stack containing the pair (root, not expanded)\\
\quad seen = an empty set\\
\quad result = an empty list\\
\quad \textbf{while} pending is not empty\\
\qquad id, expanded = pop from pending\\
\qquad \textbf{if} expanded\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad append id to result\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad continue\\
\qquad \textbf{if} id is already in seen\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad continue\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad insert id into seen\\
\qquad \textbf{if} postorder\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad push the pair (id, expanded) onto pending\\
\qquad \textbf{else}\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad append id to result\\
\qquad \textbf{for} each successor of id taken in reverse order\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad\qquad push the pair (successor, not expanded) onto pending\\
\quad \textbf{return} result
\end{pseudocode}

Three properties of this implementation are worth stating precisely,
because each of them is a decision rather than an accident.

The stack discipline determines the visit order. The successors of a node
are pushed in \emph{reverse} order, so that they pop off the stack in
forward order and are visited left to right in the order the edges were
added. A graph in which node \code{a} has successors \code{b} and
\code{c}, in that insertion order, visits \code{b} before \code{c}.

The \code{seen} set is updated at \emph{pop} time, not at push time. A node
may therefore be pushed several times --- once for each edge that reaches
it --- but it is expanded exactly once, and it appears exactly once in the
result. The cost of this choice is that the stack can hold redundant
entries; the benefit is that the marking happens at the moment the node is
actually visited, which is what makes the traversal safe in the presence of
cycles.

Cycles are cut by the \code{seen} set. A back edge to an ancestor is
simply ignored, because the ancestor has already been marked. Note the
difference from a naive recursive implementation: in the recursive version
the "visited" mark is usually placed on entry to the node, and a cycle back
to an ancestor is detected by an explicit test against the recursion stack.
Here the \code{seen} set does both jobs.

The post-order flag is pushed \emph{before} the successors, so it sits
underneath them on the stack and pops off after them. That is what makes
the post-order output correct: a node is reported only once all of its
descendants have been reported.

The two orders can be read off the output of the listing. The pre-order
traversal from \code{cli} reports \code{cli}, then \code{core}, then
\code{util}, then \code{net}. It descends from \code{cli} into its first
successor \code{core}, from \code{core} into \code{util}, and only then
backtracks to \code{cli}'s second successor \code{net}. The post-order
traversal reports the same nodes in the reverse order of their completion:
\code{util} is finished first, then \code{core}, then \code{net}, and
finally \code{cli}. A post-order traversal of a dependency graph visits a
node only after everything it depends on, which is why it is the order a
bottom-up analysis wants; a pre-order traversal is the order a top-down
analysis wants.

\section{Algorithms}

\code{topological_sort} is Kahn's algorithm. It computes the in-degree of
every live node --- the number of edges pointing into it --- and repeatedly
emits a node whose in-degree has fallen to zero, decrementing the
in-degree of its successors as it goes. The result is a vector of
identifiers in which every edge points forwards.

If the graph contains a cycle, the algorithm cannot exhaust the nodes, and
the method throws \code{std::invalid_argument} with the message
\code{"graph contains a cycle"}. Note that the check is on the \emph{number}
of nodes emitted, so a self-loop is detected just as reliably as a longer
cycle.

\code{shortest_path} is a breadth-first search that remembers the parent
of each node as it is discovered. When the target is reached, the parent
chain is walked backwards and reversed. The result is the path from source
to target inclusive, or an empty \code{std::optional} if the target is not
reachable. Because the search is breadth-first, the path found is a
shortest one in the unweighted sense; the method does not take edge
weights, and the \code{EdgeValue} payload is not consulted.

\code{strongly_connected_components} is Kosaraju's algorithm, written
iteratively. The first pass performs a depth-first traversal over every
node and records the nodes in post-order. The second pass walks that
post-order backwards and expands each component through the
\emph{predecessors} of its members --- that is, over the reversed graph.
The two passes together identify every maximal set of nodes that can reach
one another.

The implementation of the first pass is the same stack-of-pairs pattern as
\code{depth_first}, and the second pass is a breadth-first expansion over
the predecessor lists. Both are iterative, so a graph with a million nodes
in a chain will not overflow the call stack.

\section{Redirection}

\code{redirect (from, to)} is the operation that distinguishes this graph
from a plain adjacency-list container, and it is the one that
\chapref{ch:terms-and-reduction} builds on.

\begin{pseudocode}[Redirecting the incoming edges of a node]
\textbf{function} redirect (from, to)\\
\quad \textbf{if} from equals to then return\\
\quad \textbf{for} each edge e in the incoming list of from\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad set the target of e to to\\
\qquad \qquad\qquad append e to the incoming list of to\\
\quad clear the incoming list of from
\end{pseudocode}

The operation retargets the edges that point \emph{into} \code{from} so
that they point into \code{to} instead. Everything else is left alone.
Specifically:

The outgoing edges of \code{from} are untouched. \code{from} continues to
point at whatever it pointed at.

The outgoing edges of \code{to} are untouched. \code{to} does not gain any
successors.

The incoming list of \code{from} is cleared, because none of its edges
point into it any more; the incoming list of \code{to} grows by exactly
the number of retargeted edges.

The source of each retargeted edge is unchanged, so the outgoing list of
that source still correctly contains it.

Redirecting a node to itself does nothing at all.

This is precisely the operation needed to splice a replacement into a
shared structure. If a node \code{r} is being rewritten and its
replacement is a node \code{s}, then redirecting \code{r} to \code{s} makes
every parent that pointed at \code{r} point at \code{s} instead, after
which \code{r} itself can be removed. The parents' other children are
untouched, and \code{r}'s own children are discarded along with it.

It is worth walking through that pattern in detail, because it is what the
next chapter builds on. Suppose a graph has three nodes: \code{p}, with an
edge to \code{r}, and \code{r}, with an edge to \code{c}. Now suppose
\code{r} is to be replaced by a new node \code{s}. Redirecting \code{r} to
\code{s} moves the edge \code{p} to \code{r} over to \code{p} to
\code{s}: the edge's target becomes \code{s}, and the edge is appended to
\code{s}'s incoming list. The incoming list of \code{r} is now empty, and
its outgoing edge to \code{c} is untouched. Removing \code{r} then deletes
that outgoing edge and leaves \code{p} pointing at \code{s}. The parent's
own identity is unchanged, and any other edge out of \code{p} is unchanged
as well.

The children of the discarded node do not survive: the edge from \code{r}
to \code{c} is removed along with \code{r}. That is not an accident of the
implementation but a semantic decision, and it is the right one for
rewriting --- a rule that replaces a term replaces the whole term,
arguments included --- but it does mean that a graph rewrite cannot be used
to graft the children of the replaced node onto its replacement. A user
who wants that behaviour has to rewire the edges explicitly.

\section{DOT output}

\code{to_dot} produces a Graphviz DOT document. The general form takes two
callables --- one that renders a node's payload and one that renders an
edge's payload --- and a graph name:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dsldot,caption={The DOT produced by Listing~\ref{lst:ch35-graph}.},label={lst:ch35-dot}]
digraph "dependencies" {
  n0 [label="core"];
  n1 [label="net"];
  n2 [label="util"];
  n3 [label="cli"];
  n4 [label="doc"];
  n3 -> n0 [label="needs"];
  n3 -> n1 [label="needs"];
  n0 -> n2 [label="needs"];
  n1 -> n2 [label="needs"];
  n4 -> n2 [label="needs"];
}
\end{lstlisting}

The node names in the output are \code{n} followed by the identifier, so
they are stable and readable, and they correspond exactly to the
identifiers the program uses. The convenience overload
\code{to_dot (name = "G")} renders both labels by streaming the payloads
through an \code{std::ostringstream}, which means it only compiles when
both payload types are streamable.

All of the label text goes through \code{dot_quote}, a free function that
wraps the text in double quotes and escapes the characters that would
otherwise terminate or corrupt the label: a double quote becomes
\code{\textbackslash"}, a backslash becomes \code{\textbackslash\textbackslash},
a newline becomes \code{\textbackslash n} and a carriage return becomes
\code{\textbackslash r}.

There is one inconsistency in that list, and it is worth knowing about
because a label containing a tab will render differently from what one
might expect: a tab is emitted as a \emph{raw} tab character rather than as
the two-character escape \code{\textbackslash t}. Every other control
character --- anything below the space character, and the delete character
--- is rejected outright with \code{std::invalid_argument} and the message
\code{"unsupported control character in DOT label"}. Rejecting them is
better than escaping them, because DOT has no escape for them and a
silent corruption of the output would be far worse than an exception.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{warnbox}[Identifiers are never recycled]
Because identifiers are indices into a vector that only grows, a graph
that is created and destroyed repeatedly will accumulate holes. The
identifier space grows without bound even though the number of live nodes
stays small, and the vectors holding the nodes and edges never shrink. For
a graph that is built once and queried many times this is exactly what you
want. For a graph that is used as a scratch buffer in a loop, it is a
memory leak by construction.
\end{warnbox}

The remaining limits are these.

\textbf{The traversals are not generic.} \code{breadth_first} and
\code{depth_first} take a root and visit everything reachable from it.
There is no way to traverse only the edges out of a set of roots, no way to
stop early, and no visitor overload; a program that wants early exit has
to call the method and discard the tail.

\textbf{The orders are determined by edge-insertion order.} The successors
of a node come back in the order the edges were added, and the traversals
inherit that order. This is deterministic, but it means that reordering the
edges reorders the traversals, and that the traversal order is not
canonical in any sense.

\textbf{There are no edge weights.} \code{shortest_path} is a
breadth-first search, so it finds a path with the fewest edges. The
\code{EdgeValue} payload is carried but never consulted, so a weighted
shortest path has to be written by the user.

\textbf{Parallel edges are allowed and are not collapsed.} Two edges
between the same pair of nodes are two distinct edges with distinct
identifiers, and both appear in the adjacency lists. This matters for
\code{topological_sort}, whose in-degrees count edges rather than distinct
predecessors; the counting is self-consistent, but a graph with many
parallel edges has correspondingly large degrees.

\textbf{Self-loops are allowed.} A self-loop contributes one to a node's
in-degree and can never be discharged, so a graph containing one has no
topological order and \code{topological_sort} throws. In
\code{strongly_connected_components} a self-loop is harmless.

\textbf{\code{remove_node} is not atomic with respect to its edges.} It
removes the outgoing edges first and then the incoming ones, each of which
unlinks the edge from both endpoints. The observable result is correct, but
the intermediate state --- in which the node still exists and some of its
edges do not --- would be visible to a concurrent observer. The class is
not thread-safe in any case.

\textbf{\code{redirect} does not check for a cycle.} Redirecting a node
into its own descendant creates a cycle, which \code{topological_sort}
will subsequently refuse. The class performs no reachability analysis of
its own.

\textbf{There is no \code{reserve}.} The vectors grow by doubling, and
there is no way to tell the graph how many nodes it is about to hold.

\section{Closing}

\code{dsl::DirectedGraph} is a small, unremarkable graph library, and that
is its virtue. It is a free-standing class rather than a language feature,
it stores its nodes and edges in vectors of optionals so that identifiers
are stable and never recycled, and it offers exactly the traversals that a
compiler or a symbolic engine needs: breadth-first, depth-first in both
orders, topological sort, unweighted shortest path and strongly connected
components.

The operation that gives it a character of its own is \code{redirect},
which retargets a node's incoming edges without disturbing anything else.
That is the primitive that lets a graph be edited in place while other
parts of the program continue to hold identifiers to it --- and it is
exactly what the term graph of the next chapter uses to implement
rewriting over a shared structure.
